I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the framework’s analysis guidance first, then identify candidate phases and refine them in GSAS-II. I’ll keep phase identification and quantitative fractions tied to the final saved project and profile outputs.

The chemical scope is Cu–W–O from CuO + WO₃ at 800 °C. The scan has the framework’s full 10–100° grid and a relatively high, sloping background, so I’m separating peak identification from background first and will search the frozen Cu–W–O/CuO/WO₃ reference inventory before deciding the phase count.

The frozen reference index has only four Cu–W–O entries: two CuWO₄ polymorph/settings, Cu₃WO₆, and Cu₂WO₄, plus the likely precursor/decomposition phases. I’m now comparing their full peak inventories, including weak unmatched peaks—not just the strongest-line coincidences—to determine whether this is one phase or an overlapping mixture.

The line inventory supports triclinic CuWO₄ as the principal framework, but the 23–29° group contains reflections that it cannot generate; monoclinic WO₃ and possibly CuO remain plausible secondary phases. I’m moving to staged GSAS-II fits now, starting with deliberately broad profiles so the cell/offset refinement can lock onto the correct reflections before comparing candidate mixtures.

The first single-phase trial is intentionally diagnostic and has already failed physically: it lowers Rwp only by driving the cell and instrumental widths far outside the allowed generator ranges. That confirms unmodeled phases rather than a merely shifted CuWO₄ pattern. I’m stopping that over-broadened branch and will fit the chemically plausible multiphase candidates with bounded profile parameters.

The two- and three-phase fits still require 1–3% cell distortions, which is too large for the framework’s stated reference perturbations. That points to a different structural parent, not just missing CuO/WO₃. The observed 19.3/29.0/31.9/34.6° sequence is scheelite/wolframite-like, so I’m expanding the search to divalent-tungstate framework analogues and will substitute Cu only after a framework-level match is demonstrated.

The analogue search resolved the phase identity cleanly: a Cu-substituted model derived from COD 2101690 (the published Cu₀.₉Zn₀.₁WO₄ triclinic framework) gives a template correlation of 0.934 and drops the GSAS-II Rwp from ~42% to 13.5% as a single phase, with only sub-0.3% cell adjustments. I’m treating this as one CuWO₄ crystalline phase unless the refined residual reveals independent peaks.

The constrained physical refinement now reaches Rwp 6.32% with plausible calibration/profile values and no unindexed reflections. The remaining residual is intensity-shaped rather than peak-inventory-shaped, so I’m testing the framework’s permitted preferred-orientation axes and surface roughness before accepting the atomic coordinate shifts.

Profile broadening is also resolved: uniaxial microstrain along [001] improves Rwp to 5.34%, with 2329/1823 ppm components—both inside the generator’s allowed range—and is measurably better than isotropic or the other permitted axes. Surface roughness refines to allowed values; preferred orientation consistently returns to unity, so I am not including texture.

A profile-parameter degeneracy surfaced: the fitted Lorentzian width was represented as a negative instrument X plus a 0.30 µm size. Reparameterizing the identical width as X≈0.10 and size≈1.0 µm places both inside the allowed ranges. I’m also fixing polarization at its allowed lower bound (the unconstrained optimum was 0.41) before final validation, so the submitted project remains physically admissible.

The residual audit found structured positive intensity at 23.43°, 24.69°, and 34.48°—well above counting noise—so the “single phase” conclusion is not yet defensible. I’m reopening phase identification against the residual specifically; this is the right place to distinguish a minor WO₃/CuO remnant from a second CuWO₄ polymorph.

The residual search has isolated monoclinic WO₃ as the likely minor phase. I’m comparing the completed two-phase refinements now, then I’ll validate the best model by GSAS-II replay and export the final project, CIFs, profile, and report.

The best candidate is the P2₁/c WO₃ model (COD 2311041): it lowers Rwp from 4.965% to 3.876% and refines to about 2.98 wt% WO₃. Its alternative cell setting fits slightly worse. I’m now checking whether the small improvement from minor-phase coordinate/profile terms is supported rather than overfit, and whether any coherent third-phase residual remains.

The first physically constrained branch is still refining; GSAS-II is slow here because both cells, scales, profiles, the major-phase coordinates, and the 32-term background are coupled. I’ve reduced the comparison to a few decisive branches so the final project remains physically defensible.

A constrained roughness solution retains essentially the same fit (Rwp 3.881%) with valid Suortti parameters; the unconstrained negative value was just a correlated alternative parameterization. I’m keeping the physical branch and testing the last scientifically meaningful choice: whether the weak WO₃ oxygen coordinates or anisotropic broadening are actually supported.

The supported model is now stable: triclinic CuWO₄ plus monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃, with the latter at about 2.98 wt%. WO₃ oxygen-coordinate refinement is not supported (large coordinate uncertainties and a worse fit), while a [100] uniaxial width term is weakly but significantly supported. I’m freezing that restrained model and moving to replay/export validation.

The frozen project replayed successfully with zero physical-profile review flags. I’m doing the final cross-check now: CIF-to-project identity, normalized fractions, complete fit-grid agreement, and project metadata integrity.

Completed the two-phase refinement:

- Triclinic CuWO₄ (P -1): 97.02 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P 2₁/c): 2.98 wt%
- Rwp = 3.8743%, Rp = 2.9979%, GoF = 1.600

The WO₃ fraction has an estimated model-dependent uncertainty of about ±0.5 percentage points; its oxygen coordinates were not independently refinable.

